# Badges R–S (part 1): checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.reptile-and-amphibian-study
@complete complete
@note The 2025 update changed requirement 8. Venomous reptiles may not be used for the animal-care option, and collecting requires permission.
@desc Reptile and Amphibian Study explores snakes, lizards, turtles, frogs, toads and salamanders: identifying species, how reptiles and amphibians differ, their ecology and protection, venomous species, caring for or observing an animal, field skills, and separating myth from fact.
@safety Never handle venomous reptiles, and wash your hands after handling any reptile or amphibian.
1 a Identifying species :: Identifying reptile and amphibian species found in the United States.
2 a Local species :: The reptiles and amphibians found where you live.
3 k Telling them apart :: How amphibians differ from reptiles, and how frogs, toads, snakes and lizards differ.
4 k Their role in nature :: Their role in the environment and protected versus unprotected species.
5 k Food and reproduction :: What they eat and how they reproduce.
6 k How snakes move :: How snakes move without legs.
7 k! Venomous species :: The venomous snakes and lizard found in the United States.
8 a Care or observation :: One option: caring for an animal for a month, or observing one at a zoo or exhibit over time.
9 a Field skills :: Two options, such as identifying species by voice or sight.
10 d Myths and facts :: Common superstitions and surprising true facts about reptiles and amphibians.

@owner mb.rifle-shooting
@note Rifle Shooting has general requirements and a choice of modern cartridge, air rifle or muzzleloader options, each with its own marksmanship standards. The excerpts used inconsistent numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Rifle Shooting teaches firearm safety and marksmanship: safety at home and on the range, laws and hunting ethics, rifle parts and care, shooting fundamentals, and qualifying with a modern rifle, air rifle or muzzleloader under a certified instructor.
@safety Shooting is done only on an approved range under a certified range officer and instructor, following the National Range and Target Activities Manual.

@owner mb.robotics
@complete complete
@note The 2026 update added a careers-or-hobby choice as requirement 7.
@desc Robotics introduces how robots sense, think and move: safety, kinds of robots and how they move, the major fields of robotics, designing, building, programming and testing your own robot, competitions, and careers or hobbies.
1 k! Safety :: Hazards of working with robots, protective gear and first aid.
2 k Robot basics :: What robots do, remote-controlled versus autonomous robots, and ways robots move.
3 k Fields of robotics :: Several major fields of robotics and why they matter.
4 a Building a robot :: Designing, building, programming and testing a robot, with an engineering notebook.
5 demo Demonstrating your robot :: Showing your robot and reviewing your notebook with your counselor.
6 a Competitions :: One option: attending a robotics competition, or researching youth competitions.
7 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: robotics careers, or a hobby that uses these skills.

@owner mb.rowing
@note The official page was updated in 2025, but the cached text appeared to be an earlier version, so no requirement outline is published yet. Topics include safety and first aid, the swimmer test, life jackets, launching, rowing and landing, swamped-boat and rescue drills, and boat and oar knowledge.
@desc Rowing teaches safe handling of a rowboat: hazards and first aid, the swimmer test and life jackets, launching, rowing a course and landing, swamped-boat and rescue drills, and the boats, oars and techniques of rowing.
@safety Rowing follows Safety Afloat with a qualified supervisor; pass the swimmer test and wear a life jacket.

@owner mb.safety
@note Excerpts were fragmentary and did not show current numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet. Topics include managing hazards, home and family safety, crime and personal safety, emergencies in public places, and transportation safety.
@desc Safety is about recognizing and reducing risk: what safety means, home inspections and escape plans, personal and online safety, what to do in emergencies in public places, and safety as a pedestrian and passenger.

@owner mb.salesmanship
@complete complete
@desc Salesmanship explores how good salespeople serve customers: their responsibilities, researching markets and products, writing a sales plan, giving a presentation, a real sales project, interviewing salespeople, and sales careers.
1 k What salespeople do :: The responsibilities of salespeople and business versus consumer sales.
2 k Preparing to sell :: Researching the market, knowing the product and following up after a sale.
3 a A sales plan :: Writing and presenting a sales plan for an assigned product and territory.
4 a A sales presentation :: Giving a sales presentation for an assigned product.
5 a A sales project :: One option, such as unit fundraising, selling services or retail selling, with a cost record.
6 a Interviews :: Interviewing a salesperson and a store owner.
7 a Careers :: Researching careers in sales.

@owner mb.scholarship
@complete complete
@desc Scholarship recognizes effort in school: good or improving grades, exploring education and careers, a note on your conduct and service, teamwork in school activities, and reflecting on how school prepares you for the future.
1 a Your grades :: Showing a strong average for a term, or improvement over the previous period.
2 a Education and careers :: Two options, such as visiting an educational place or interviewing professionals about their training.
3 a A note on your conduct :: A note from a school official, or a parent if homeschooled, about your behavior, leadership and service.
4 a Teamwork :: One option: a school activity and what it taught you about teamwork, or a team project.
5 a A written report :: A short report on how school will help your future or your career interests.

@owner mb.scouting-heritage
@complete partial
@note Requirement 5 was not clearly visible in the excerpts and is not outlined yet.
@desc Scouting Heritage explores the history of Scouting: the people who started it, key places and events, Scouting memorabilia and museums, the history of your own unit or council, old-time games, and interviewing former Scouts.
1 k Founders :: People who shaped early Scouting in the United States.
2 k Key moments :: The importance of places and events such as Brownsea Island.
3 a A Scouting exhibit :: Visiting an exhibit of Scouting memorabilia or history.
4 a Your unit’s history :: Researching your unit, council, camp or lodge and presenting what you learn.
6 a A collection :: A collection of patches or other Scouting items.
7 a An old-time game :: Recreating and teaching an old-time game like those played at Brownsea Island.
8 a Interviews :: Interviewing former Scouts about their experiences.
9 a Careers or hobbies :: One option related to careers or hobbies.
